1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"phase-2--2b-develop-your-comprehension","kind":"phase-article","route":"/roadmap/phase-2/2b-develop-your-comprehension","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/phase-2/2b-develop-your-comprehension","sourceHash":"1210d30eb4029624ab048bdf3efce26a96ec17eeb50f1ad7447a7e0e1df37176","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T23:11:44.163Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"2B — Develop your Comprehension","zh":"2B——提升你的理解能力"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"what-youre-doing","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"What You're Doing","zh":"你正在做什么"}},{"id":"segment-0002","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"You know the 2000 most common words, and you can follow a lot of what's happening in native content when you have tools to help. Now it's time to deepen that comprehension and start learning vocabulary more efficiently.","zh":"你已经掌握了最常见的 2000 个词，在有工具帮助时，也能跟上原生内容中许多正在发生的事情。现在是时候加深理解能力，并开始更高效地学习词汇了。"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Up to this point, you've been learning words from a frequency list — the most common words regardless of topic. But beyond 2000 words, the \"next most common\" words depend heavily on <em>what</em> you're reading or watching. A medical drama has different vocabulary than a cooking show, which has different vocabulary than a fantasy novel. The words you need to learn next should come from the content you actually enjoy. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/domains\">Domains</a>","zh":"到目前为止，你一直在根据词频表学习词汇——不论主题，学习的都是最常见的词。但超过 2000 个词之后，“下一个最常见的词”在很大程度上取决于你正在<em>阅读或观看的内容</em>。医学剧的词汇不同于烹饪节目，而烹饪节目的词汇又不同于奇幻小说。接下来需要学习的词，应该来自你真正喜欢的内容。 <a href=\"/roadmap/library/domains\">领域</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This is where sentence mining comes in. Instead of studying a generic word list, you'll be pulling words directly from your immersion and studying them in context. This is one of the most powerful techniques in the Refold method, and for many learners, it becomes their favorite activity.","zh":"句子挖掘就是在这时发挥作用。你不再学习通用词表，而是直接从沉浸材料中提取词汇，并在语境中学习。这是 Refold 方法中最有效的技巧之一，对许多学习者来说，它最终会成为最喜欢的活动。"}},{"id":"whats-new","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"What's New","zh":"有什么新内容"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Sentence Mining</strong> — The core new technique. While immersing in a TV show or video with target-language subtitles, you pause when you encounter a sentence with one or two unknown words. You look up the words, make sure you understand the sentence, and save it as a flashcard for later review. Over time, this builds a personalized vocabulary deck drawn entirely from content you care about. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/sentence-mining\">Sentence Mining</a>","zh":"<strong>句子挖掘</strong>——核心的新技巧。在观看带有目标语言字幕的电视剧或视频时，遇到包含一两个生词的句子就暂停。查出这些词，确认自己理解了整个句子，然后将其保存为闪卡，以便之后复习。随着时间推移，这会建立起一套完全取自你所关注内容的个性化词汇牌组。 <a href=\"/roadmap/library/sentence-mining\">句子挖掘</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0007","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The workflow takes some time to set up — there are tools involved and it can feel clunky at first. But once you've got the hang of it, the process becomes smooth and almost addictive. The key insight is that you're not trying to mine every unknown word. You're looking for \"low-hanging fruit\" — sentences where you already understand most of the words and only need to learn one or two new ones.","zh":"这套流程需要一些时间来设置——其中涉及各种工具，刚开始可能会觉得不够顺手。但一旦熟悉之后，整个过程会变得流畅，甚至近乎令人上瘾。关键在于，你不是要挖掘每一个生词，而是在寻找“低垂的果实”——也就是那些你已经理解大多数词、只需学习一两个新词的句子。"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Domain focus</strong> — Rather than trying to understand everything in the language, narrow your immersion to a few related domains (types of content). If you love cooking shows, watch cooking shows. If you like slice-of-life dramas, lean into those. By focusing on a domain, you'll encounter the same vocabulary repeatedly, which means you'll learn it faster and reach comfortable comprehension sooner. Once you've mastered one domain, branching into others is much easier because a lot of vocabulary overlaps. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/domains\">Domains</a>","zh":"<strong>聚焦领域</strong>——不要试图理解这门语言中的所有内容，而是将沉浸范围缩小到几个相关领域（内容类型）。如果你喜欢烹饪节目，就看烹饪节目。如果你喜欢日常生活类电视剧，就多接触这类内容。聚焦某个领域后，你会反复遇到相同的词汇，从而学得更快，也能更早达到舒适的理解程度。掌握一个领域后，再拓展到其他领域会容易得多，因为不同领域之间有大量词汇重叠。 <a href=\"/roadmap/library/domains\">领域</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Pure reading (optional)</strong> — If you enjoy reading, this is a decent time to start. Graded readers (stories written for learners at specific levels) work great, but many learners find them boring. Book series aimed at younger readers are often a better choice — they're written for native speakers, so the language is natural, but the vocabulary and sentence structure are simpler than adult novels. Reading a series by the same author also helps because you get used to their style and learn the recurring vocabulary. If you're struggling to read without audio, listening along to an audiobook or narration is a fantastic alternative. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/narrow-reading\">Narrow Reading</a>","zh":"<strong>纯阅读（可选）</strong>——如果你喜欢阅读，现在是开始阅读的不错时机。分级读物（为特定水平的学习者编写的故事）效果很好，但许多学习者觉得它们很无聊。面向年龄较小读者的系列书通常是更好的选择——它们是为母语者写的，因此语言自然，但词汇和句子结构比成人小说简单。阅读同一作者的系列作品也很有帮助，因为你会逐渐熟悉作者的风格，并学会反复出现的词汇。如果没有音频就难以阅读，可以一边听有声书或旁白，这是非常好的替代方案。 <a href=\"/roadmap/library/narrow-reading\">窄式阅读</a>"}},{"id":"your-routine","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Your Routine","zh":"你的日常安排"}},{"id":"segment-0011","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Your time shifts slightly as sentence mining combines priming and interactive immersion: <a href=\"/roadmap/library/the-pillars-of-language-learning\">The Pillars of Language Learning</a>","zh":"由于句子挖掘结合了预热和互动沉浸，你的时间分配会略有变化： <a href=\"/roadmap/library/the-pillars-of-language-learning\">语言学习的支柱</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"<strong>Priming:</strong> 20%","zh":"<strong>预热：</strong> 20%"},{"en":"<strong>Interactive Immersion:</strong> 50%","zh":"<strong>互动沉浸：</strong> 50%"},{"en":"<strong>Freeflow Immersion:</strong> 30%","zh":"<strong>自由流沉浸：</strong> 30%"}]},{"id":"segment-0013","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Sentence mining is inherently interactive (you're pausing, looking things up, saving words), but the flashcard review it produces counts as priming. This tight integration is what makes it so effective.","zh":"句子挖掘本身具有互动性（你会暂停、查词并保存词汇），但由此产生的闪卡复习属于预热。这种紧密结合正是它如此有效的原因。"}},{"id":"sample-daily-schedule","type":"heading","level":3,"content":{"en":"Sample Daily Schedule","zh":"每日安排示例"}},{"id":"segment-0015","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"Vocab study (Anki reviews + mined cards): 20 mins","zh":"词汇学习（Anki 复习 + 挖掘出的卡片）：20 分钟"},{"en":"Sentence Mining with a TV show: 45 mins","zh":"使用电视剧进行句子挖掘：45 分钟"},{"en":"Freeflow Reading with Audio: 25 mins","zh":"带音频的自由流阅读：25 分钟"}]},{"id":"when-to-move-on","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"When to Move On","zh":"何时进入下一阶段"}},{"id":"segment-0017","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Move to 2C when you:","zh":"满足以下条件时，进入 2C："}},{"id":"segment-0018","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"Know around 3000 words","zh":"大约掌握 3000 个词"},{"en":"Can comfortably follow an adult TV show using subtitles, with only light use of tools (occasional lookups rather than constant pausing)","zh":"使用字幕观看成人电视剧时，只需少量使用工具（偶尔查词，而不是不断暂停），就能轻松跟上内容"}]},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与依据"}},{"id":"segment-0020","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Sentence mining aligns with research on incidental vocabulary acquisition. <a href=\"https://www.routledge.com/The-Routledge-Handbook-of-Vocabulary-Studies/Webb/p/book/9781032401270\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Webb (2020)</a> extensively documented that learners acquire vocabulary incidentally through exposure to meaningful input — exactly what happens when you mine sentences from content you're watching. Learning words in their actual context within engaging content increases retention compared to disconnected word lists.","zh":"句子挖掘与关于附带词汇习得的研究相一致。<a href=\"https://www.routledge.com/The-Routledge-Handbook-of-Vocabulary-Studies/Webb/p/book/9781032401270\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Webb（2020）</a>详细记录了学习者如何通过接触有意义的输入附带习得词汇——从所观看内容中挖掘句子时，正是这一过程。相比脱离语境的词表，在引人入胜的内容中学习实际语境里的词汇，能提高记忆保持率。"}},{"id":"segment-0021","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The emphasis on domain focus draws from research on narrow reading. <a href=\"http://www.sdkrashen.com/content/books/the_power_of_reading.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Krashen (2004)</a> argued that reading extensively within a limited domain — same author, same genre, same topic area — accelerates vocabulary acquisition because learners encounter the same vocabulary repeatedly in natural contexts. Meta-analytic research supports the broader approach: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/tesq.157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nakanishi (2015)</a> synthesized 34 empirical studies and found that extensive reading produces consistent positive effects on reading proficiency, with learners who engage with themed, self-selected content showing stronger gains and higher motivation.","zh":"聚焦领域的做法源自关于窄式阅读的研究。<a href=\"http://www.sdkrashen.com/content/books/the_power_of_reading.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Krashen（2004）</a>认为，在有限领域内进行广泛阅读——同一作者、同一体裁或同一主题范围——能够加快词汇习得，因为学习者会在自然语境中反复遇到相同的词汇。元分析研究支持这一更广泛的方法：<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/tesq.157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nakanishi（2015）</a>综合了 34 项实证研究，发现广泛阅读能持续对阅读能力产生积极影响；接触主题明确、由自己选择的内容的学习者，进步更明显，动机也更强。"}}],"cards":[]},"siteBasePath":"/my_learning_docs"}]
